---
import { REPO_URL } from "../../config";
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";

const build = `git clone ${REPO_URL}.git emojisense
cd emojisense
pnpm install
pnpm data:build`;

const embed = `npx wrangler login
pnpm --filter @emojisense/data embed -- --models embeddinggemma --dims 256`;

const sync = "pnpm --filter @emojisense/worker sync -- --model embeddinggemma --dims 256";

const deploy = "pnpm --filter @emojisense/worker deploy";

const client = `const sense = useEmojisense({
  packBaseUrl: "https://emojisense-api.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev/v1/pack/0.1.0",
  endpoint: "https://emojisense-api.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev",
});`;

const offline = `pnpm --filter @emojisense/worker sync -- --placeholder
pnpm --filter @emojisense/worker dev:offline`;
---

<Docs path="/docs/self-host/" source="README.md">
  <h2 id="what-you-run">What you run</h2>
  <p>
    The search API is one Cloudflare Worker (<code>packages/worker</code>). It serves the data packs as static
    files, keeps the emoji vectors in its own bundle, embeds queries with Workers AI and caches answers per data
    center. There are no containers, load balancers or vector databases to run.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything here is MIT. You call the embedding models on your own account, under each model’s license.
    The model weights are never part of the repository.
  </p>

  <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Node.js 24 and pnpm 9.</li>
    <li>
      A Cloudflare account with Workers AI. The free allowance is 10,000 neurons a day. For production traffic,
      use Workers Paid ($5 a month).
    </li>
    <li><code>wrangler login</code> on the machine that embeds the emoji and deploys the Worker.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="steps">Steps</h2>
  <h3 id="build-the-pack">1. Build the data pack</h3>
  <p>This joins Emojibase, Unicode CLDR and the alias files into the locale packs.</p>
  <pre class="code"><code>{build}</code></pre>

  <h3 id="embed">2. Embed the emoji on your account</h3>
  <p>
    This sends the 1,914 emoji descriptions to Workers AI in batches and writes the vector file. Pick one
    model and one size, and keep them: queries and emoji must use the same model at the same size.
  </p>
  <pre class="code"><code>{embed}</code></pre>

  <h3 id="sync">3. Copy the pack and vectors into the Worker</h3>
  <pre class="code"><code>{sync}</code></pre>

  <h3 id="configure">4. Configure</h3>
  <p>Edit <code>packages/worker/wrangler.jsonc</code> before the first deploy:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>name</code>: the Worker name, which is also the <code>workers.dev</code> subdomain.</li>
    <li><code>vars.PUBLISHABLE_KEYS</code>: a comma-separated list of the keys your apps may send.</li>
    <li><code>ratelimits</code>: requests per minute for keyed and anonymous callers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="deploy">5. Deploy</h3>
  <pre class="code"><code>{deploy}</code></pre>

  <h3 id="point-clients">6. Point your apps at it</h3>
  <pre class="code"><code>{client}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="dashboard">Keys and usage (optional)</h2>
  <p>
    The dashboard (<code>apps/dashboard</code>) issues keys, binds them to origins and shows usage per month.
    It runs on Cloudflare too, with a D1 database. The schema lives in <code>packages/platform/migrations</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="without-cloudflare">Try it without a Cloudflare account</h2>
  <p>
    Workers AI has no local emulator. The offline mode starts the Worker with an empty vector file, so it
    answers with dictionary results only and marks them <code>degraded: true</code>.
  </p>
  <pre class="code"><code>{offline}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="what-hosted-adds">What the hosted service adds</h2>
  <p>
    Self-hosted data is the open pack, refreshed every quarter. The hosted API also gets daily alias updates
    learned from searches that missed, photo to emoji, custom emoji hosting and analytics, with no servers or
    model calls to run yourself. See <a href="/pricing/">pricing</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
